      overview: "A credit document/debt cannot generally be sold at a price other than its nominal (face) value; even at face value, sale of debt is valid only with recourse to the original debtor, as under Hawalah.",
      simpleExplanation: "You generally can't sell a debt someone owes you -- like an invoice or a bill of exchange -- to someone else, especially not at a discount or premium, because it's too uncertain whether the original debtor will actually pay. The only way to legitimately hand a debt to someone else is through Hawalah, where the new holder can still go after the original debtor if something goes wrong.",
      academicExplanation: "A credit document (bill of exchange, export bill, promissory note) arising from a credit sale represents a debt, which cannot be sold under Shari'ah rules due to Gharar and/or Riba -- a trader cannot sell such a document to an Islamic bank the way he could to a conventional bank. As an alternative, the bank can act as a genuine trader, purchasing the underlying commodity from its producer and reselling it to those needing credit, keeping its own margin. The OIC Fiqh Academy and Shari'ah scholars generally regard sale/purchase of debt-representing securities/documents at any price other than face value as incompatible with Shari'ah; even at face value, sale of debt is valid only where the purchaser has recourse to the original debtor, as under Hawalah. The Fiqh maxim 'Al-Kali bil-Kali' (also termed Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn) forbids exchange of two delayed counter-values, prevalent among pre-Islamic Arabs -- specifically prohibiting a person who cannot pay a due credit debt from asking to 'sell it to me on credit for a further period, for something additional' (the Prophet reportedly prohibited this, and it carries near-universal Ijma'a authority). The best contemporary example of this prohibited practice is 'rollover' in Murabaha: when a client defaults, banks enter a fresh Murabaha to extend time and charge more on the receivable -- all Shari'ah boards prohibit this and any resulting return is illegitimate income. In early-1980s Pakistan, banks were briefly permitted to purchase trade bills as a form of Murabaha, but the Council of Islamic Ideology and Shari'ah Courts disapproved this practice; Islamic banks should not trade in such securities/debts since debt instruments are not saleable at a premium or discount. The key legal distinction: 'sale of debt' (prohibited) differs from 'assignment of debt' via Hawalah (permissible) in that Hawalah provides recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee (new creditor) is not paid. Sukuk certificates, when genuinely backed by and evidencing ownership of a real asset (via securitization), transform into objects of value and become validly tradeable in primary and secondary markets, provided returns are tied to the cash flow of the underlying asset. Semi-debt instruments like leasing contracts (which obligate the user to specific rent commitments) resemble debt but can be traded under certain conditions since such trading represents sale of the leased asset itself at negotiated prices.",
      examExplanation: "Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) = generally prohibited (Gharar/Riba) -- can't sell a bill of exchange, export bill, or promissory note to an Islamic bank at a premium/discount. Even at face value, valid only with recourse to the original debtor (as in Hawalah). Al-Kali bil-Kali maxim: forbids exchange of two delayed counter-values (Bai' al-Dayn bid-Dayn) -- classic example being demanding a further-delayed, higher price when a credit debtor can't pay on time. Modern example of the prohibited practice: Murabaha 'rollover' on default -- prohibited by all Shari'ah boards. Sukuk are different: if genuinely asset-backed (not pure debt-representation), they ARE tradeable, since their cash flow derives from a real underlying asset.",
